WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.406
Django applications often expose
REST-style endpoints, internal APIs,

2
00:00:06.486 --> 00:00:11.915
webhooks, background-triggered views, or
service integrations.

3
00:00:11.995 --> 00:00:16.949
API security starts with the same
principles as web security:

4
00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:22.744
authenticate the caller, validate the
request, authorize the action,

5
00:00:22.824 --> 00:00:26.920
protect sensitive data, and handle errors
safely.

6
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.191
Token validation concepts include checking
issuer, audience, expiration,

7
00:00:33.271 --> 00:00:38.510
signature or integrity, and intended
context where applicable.

8
00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:44.782
Rate limiting and throttling at a high
level can reduce abuse and operational

9
00:00:44.862 --> 00:00:48.672
strain, but they do not replace
authorization.

10
00:00:48.752 --> 00:00:54.563
Third-party integrations and webhooks add
trust-boundary questions.

11
00:00:54.643 --> 00:01:00.263
The application should know which partner
or service is sending data,

12
00:01:00.343 --> 00:01:03.391
how request authenticity is checked,

13
00:01:03.471 --> 00:01:09.091
how replay or duplicate delivery is
handled, what happens on retries,

14
00:01:09.171 --> 00:01:13.457
and which actions are safe to perform
automatically.

15
00:01:13.537 --> 00:01:17.443
Outbound integrations should protect
secrets,

16
00:01:17.523 --> 00:01:23.048
constrain destinations where appropriate,
use sensible timeouts,

17
00:01:23.128 --> 00:01:29.986
and handle failures without exposing
sensitive internals or creating repeated side

18
00:01:30.066 --> 00:01:30.828
effects.

19
00:01:30.908 --> 00:01:37.385
Dependency hygiene is part of Django
security because the application relies on

20
00:01:37.465 --> 00:01:44.038
Django itself, Python packages,
middleware, admin extensions, API frameworks,

21
00:01:44.118 --> 00:01:50.595
image libraries, parsers, container
images, and operating system components.

22
00:01:50.675 --> 00:01:57.057
Pinning concepts, vulnerability scanning,
secrets scanning, update review,

23
00:01:57.137 --> 00:02:03.615
and release testing help teams understand
what they run and how risk changes over

24
00:02:03.695 --> 00:02:04.171
time.

25
00:02:04.251 --> 00:02:09.109
Alerts should be triaged based on actual
exposure and usage,

26
00:02:09.189 --> 00:02:12.142
not ignored and not applied blindly.

27
00:02:12.222 --> 00:02:16.699
Security-focused tests and monitoring
close the loop.

28
00:02:16.779 --> 00:02:22.589
Unit and integration tests should cover
authentication requirements,

29
00:02:22.669 --> 00:02:28.004
authorization boundaries, CSRF behavior,
session transitions,

30
00:02:28.084 --> 00:02:33.132
validation failures, file upload limits,
and error behavior.

31
00:02:33.212 --> 00:02:38.165
Negative tests prove that forbidden
actions stay forbidden.

32
00:02:38.245 --> 00:02:44.723
Safe logging should capture important
audit events without exposing secrets or

33
00:02:44.803 --> 00:02:47.375
sensitive data unnecessarily.

34
00:02:47.455 --> 00:02:53.646
Monitoring and incident readiness help the
team see when assumptions fail in

35
00:02:53.726 --> 00:02:57.060
production and respond with confidence.
